    {
      "id": "EJBM_PWD_bHO2Wrp5E96t",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 588.9387146898734,
      "y": 18724.012612313432,
      "width": 198,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 415648964,
      "version": 8,
      "versionNonce": 1621747652,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789657412318,
      "created": 1789657396706,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Part 1: Page Cache",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Part 1: Page Cache",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "8MlU_-DiZbdrNHU3bdNWZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 620.9543396898734,
      "y": 18768.278237313432,
      "width": 1188,
      "height": 1075,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 324663164,
      "version": 98,
      "versionNonce": 576061252,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789657816420,
      "created": 1789657414089,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Before we move to Docker/Kubernetes, let's understand one important Linux behavior:\nLinux uses unused RAM as a cache to make disk access faster.\n\nLinux keeps frequently\n used file data in RAM\n\nFor example, suppose your application repeatedly reads:\n\nconfig.json\nusers.csv\nsome_library.so\n\nInstead of going to the SSD every single time: Linux can keep recently/frequently accessed file data in RAM:\n\nApplication\n     ↓\nRAM (page cache)\n     ↓\nMuch faster\n\nSo if you see Linux using a lot of RAM, that doesn't automatically mean applications are \nconsuming all of it.\n\nSome of that RAM may be serving as cache and can be reclaimed when applications need memory.\n\nSimple example\n\nImagine: 16 GB RAM\nYour applications use: 8 GB\n\nLinux may use another: 5 GB → filesystem cache\nand perhaps only: 3 GB → currently unused\n\nThat's generally fine.\nIf your application suddenly needs another 2 GB, Linux can reclaim appropriate cache memory.\n\n🧠 Remember this\n\nFree RAM is wasted RAM, so Linux tries to use spare RAM as cache.\nThat's why yesterday we discussed:\nfree -h\n\nand why available is more useful than simply looking at free.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Before we move to Docker/Kubernetes, let's understand one important Linux behavior:\nLinux uses unused RAM as a cache to make disk access faster.\n\nLinux keeps frequently\n used file data in RAM\n\nFor example, suppose your application repeatedly reads:\n\nconfig.json\nusers.csv\nsome_library.so\n\nInstead of going to the SSD every single time: Linux can keep recently/frequently accessed file data in RAM:\n\nApplication\n     ↓\nRAM (page cache)\n     ↓\nMuch faster\n\nSo if you see Linux using a lot of RAM, that doesn't automatically mean applications are \nconsuming all of it.\n\nSome of that RAM may be serving as cache and can be reclaimed when applications need memory.\n\nSimple example\n\nImagine: 16 GB RAM\nYour applications use: 8 GB\n\nLinux may use another: 5 GB → filesystem cache\nand perhaps only: 3 GB → currently unused\n\nThat's generally fine.\nIf your application suddenly needs another 2 GB, Linux can reclaim appropriate cache memory.\n\n🧠 Remember this\n\nFree RAM is wasted RAM, so Linux tries to use spare RAM as cache.\nThat's why yesterday we discussed:\nfree -h\n\nand why available is more useful than simply looking at free.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "eyg3aj4VeYNzCWCIr9T0l",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 570.9387146898734,
      "y": 19906.012612313432,
      "width": 143,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5N",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 316273860,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 2091875140,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789657859706,
      "created": 1789657858193,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
