      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We already know:\n\nSSD → stores files\nRAM → working memory\nCPU → executes instructions\n\nNow imagine your machine has 8 GB RAM.\nYour applications currently need:\n\nRAM needed = 9 GB\nRAM available = 8 GB\n\nWhat can Linux do?\nThis is where Swap comes in.\n\n\n1. What is Swap?\n\nSwap is basically:\nA space on your SSD/disk that Linux can use when it needs to move some memory data out of RAM.\nThink of your RAM as your desk.\nYour SSD is your cupboard.\nYour desk is small:\n\nRAM\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│ File A                  │\n│ File B                  │\n│ File C                  │\n│ File D                  │\n└─────────────────────────┘\n\nNow the desk is full.\nYou don't need File A right now.\nSo you put File A into the cupboard:\n\nRAM                         SSD / Swap\n┌──────────────────┐        ┌──────────────┐\n│ File B           │        │ File A       │\n│ File C           │  ←───  │              │\n│ File D           │        └──────────────┘\n└──────────────────┘\n\nNow RAM has some space.\n\nThat is roughly what swapping means.\n\n\n2. Why do we need Swap?\n\nSuppose: RAM = 8 GB\n\nApplications suddenly need more memory.\nWithout any mechanism:\n\nRAM\n8 GB\n████████████████\nFULL\n\nLinux needs another option.\nIt can move some less-active memory pages to swap:\n\nRAM                     Swap\n██████████████          ██\nActive pages            Less-active pages\n\nThen RAM becomes available for something else.\n\n\n\n3. Is Swap as fast as RAM?\n\nNo.\nThis is extremely important.\n\nRAM: VERY FAST\nSSD: MUCH SLOWER than RAM\n\nSo:\n\nRAM\n ↓\nFast\n\nSwap on SSD\n ↓\nMuch slower\n\nThat's why swap is not a replacement for having enough RAM.\nIt's more like a safety mechanism / overflow area.\n\n\n4. What exactly gets moved?\n\nRemember our previous lesson about pages?\nVirtual Memory\n┌─────────┐\n│ Page 1  │\n├─────────┤\n│ Page 2  │\n├─────────┤\n│ Page 3  │\n├─────────┤\n│ Page 4  │\n└─────────┘\n\nLinux manages memory in pages.\nSome pages may not be actively needed right now.\nLinux can move appropriate pages from RAM to swap.\n\nRAM                         Swap\n┌─────────┐                ┌─────────┐\n│ Page 1   │                │ Page 4  │\n│ Page 2   │ ────────────> │         │\n│ Page 3   │                └─────────┘\n└─────────┘\n\nSo remember:\n\nSwap works with memory pages.\n\n\n5. What happens if the application needs that page again?\n\nSuppose: Page 4\nwas moved to swap.\nLater the application needs it.\nLinux says:\n\"I need Page 4.\"\nBut Page 4 isn't currently in RAM.\nSo Linux brings it back:\n\nSwap\n  │\n  │ Page 4\n  ↓\nRAM\n\nThis is called a page fault in the relevant case: the process references a virtual page that isn't currently resident in physical RAM, so the OS must resolve it.\nFor now, don't worry about the different types of page faults.\nJust remember:\n\nPage needed\n     ↓\nNot in RAM\n     ↓\nGet it from disk/swap\n     ↓\nPut it into RAM\n     ↓\nContinue execution\n\nBecause disk is slower than RAM, lots of swapping can make the machine very slow.\n\n6. Check Swap on Linux\nRun: free -h\n\nYou may see:\n\n               total   used   free\nMem:            16Gi    10Gi   1Gi\nSwap:            4Gi     0Gi   4Gi\n\nHere:\n\nRAM  = 16 GB\nSwap = 4 GB\n\n\nYou can also run:\nswapon --show \nThis shows configured swap areas.\n\n\n7. Important interview question\n\"If my server has 16 GB RAM and 4 GB swap, does it have 20 GB RAM?\"\nNo.\n\nIt has:\nPhysical RAM = 16 GB\nSwap         = 4 GB\n\nSwap is disk-backed storage, not physical RAM.\nThink:\n\n16 GB actual desk\n+\n4 GB cupboard\n\n≠\n\n20 GB desk\n\n\n8. Now the important production scenario\nImagine your Django application is running on an EC2 server.\n\nEC2\n┌──────────────────────┐\n│ RAM = 8 GB           │\n│                      │\n│ Django               │\n│ Nginx                │\n│ Celery               │\n│ Other processes      │\n└──────────────────────┘\n\n\nSuddenly Django starts consuming a lot of memory.\nLinux may first try to manage memory pressure using reclaimable memory, including filesystem cache, and potentially swap if configured and appropriate.\nBut suppose memory pressure becomes extreme.\nEventually:\n\nRAM\n████████████████\nVERY HIGH PRESSURE\n\nAnd Linux cannot satisfy an allocation.\n\nNow we reach our next concept:\n\n🚨 OOM Killer\nOOM = Out Of Memory\n\n\nThe OOM killer is Linux's mechanism for terminating processes when the system cannot satisfy memory demands.\n\nConceptually:\n\n\nApplication needs memory\n        ↓\nLinux tries to reclaim memory\n        ↓\nSwap may be used if available/configured\n        ↓\nStill cannot satisfy memory allocation\n        ↓\nOOM situation\n        ↓\nLinux may kill a process\n\nThe goal is basically to recover enough memory so the system can continue operating.\n\n\n🧠 Don't memorize the details yet\n\nFor today, make sure these are crystal clear:\n\nPage Cache\n\nSpare RAM can be used to cache file data to make future disk reads faster.\n\nSwap\n\nDisk space used to hold some memory pages when RAM is under pressure.\n\nOOM\n\nThe system cannot satisfy memory allocation demands, so Linux may kill a process to recover memory.\n\nAnd the overall picture:\n\n\n                RAM\n                 │\n        ┌────────┴────────┐\n        │                 │\n   Application       Page Cache\n        │\n        ↓\n   Memory pressure\n        │\n        ↓\n      Swap\n        │\n        ↓\n   Still insufficient?\n        │\n        ↓\n    OOM Killer\n\n\nNext step\n\nNow we can connect this directly to Docker:\n\nLinux memory\n     ↓\nDocker container\n     ↓\nContainer memory limit\n     ↓\ncgroups\n     ↓\nContainer gets OOMKilled\n\nThat's where these concepts become very practical for a backend/DevOps engineer.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We already know:\n\nSSD → stores files\nRAM → working memory\nCPU → executes instructions\n\nNow imagine your machine has 8 GB RAM.\nYour applications currently need:\n\nRAM needed = 9 GB\nRAM available = 8 GB\n\nWhat can Linux do?\nThis is where Swap comes in.\n\n\n1. What is Swap?\n\nSwap is basically:\nA space on your SSD/disk that Linux can use when it needs to move some memory data out of RAM.\nThink of your RAM as your desk.\nYour SSD is your cupboard.\nYour desk is small:\n\nRAM\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│ File A                  │\n│ File B                  │\n│ File C                  │\n│ File D                  │\n└─────────────────────────┘\n\nNow the desk is full.\nYou don't need File A right now.\nSo you put File A into the cupboard:\n\nRAM                         SSD / Swap\n┌──────────────────┐        ┌──────────────┐\n│ File B           │        │ File A       │\n│ File C           │  ←───  │              │\n│ File D           │        └──────────────┘\n└──────────────────┘\n\nNow RAM has some space.\n\nThat is roughly what swapping means.\n\n\n2. Why do we need Swap?\n\nSuppose: RAM = 8 GB\n\nApplications suddenly need more memory.\nWithout any mechanism:\n\nRAM\n8 GB\n████████████████\nFULL\n\nLinux needs another option.\nIt can move some less-active memory pages to swap:\n\nRAM                     Swap\n██████████████          ██\nActive pages            Less-active pages\n\nThen RAM becomes available for something else.\n\n\n\n3. Is Swap as fast as RAM?\n\nNo.\nThis is extremely important.\n\nRAM: VERY FAST\nSSD: MUCH SLOWER than RAM\n\nSo:\n\nRAM\n ↓\nFast\n\nSwap on SSD\n ↓\nMuch slower\n\nThat's why swap is not a replacement for having enough RAM.\nIt's more like a safety mechanism / overflow area.\n\n\n4. What exactly gets moved?\n\nRemember our previous lesson about pages?\nVirtual Memory\n┌─────────┐\n│ Page 1  │\n├─────────┤\n│ Page 2  │\n├─────────┤\n│ Page 3  │\n├─────────┤\n│ Page 4  │\n└─────────┘\n\nLinux manages memory in pages.\nSome pages may not be actively needed right now.\nLinux can move appropriate pages from RAM to swap.\n\nRAM                         Swap\n┌─────────┐                ┌─────────┐\n│ Page 1   │                │ Page 4  │\n│ Page 2   │ ────────────> │         │\n│ Page 3   │                └─────────┘\n└─────────┘\n\nSo remember:\n\nSwap works with memory pages.\n\n\n5. What happens if the application needs that page again?\n\nSuppose: Page 4\nwas moved to swap.\nLater the application needs it.\nLinux says:\n\"I need Page 4.\"\nBut Page 4 isn't currently in RAM.\nSo Linux brings it back:\n\nSwap\n  │\n  │ Page 4\n  ↓\nRAM\n\nThis is called a page fault in the relevant case: the process references a virtual page that isn't currently resident in physical RAM, so the OS must resolve it.\nFor now, don't worry about the different types of page faults.\nJust remember:\n\nPage needed\n     ↓\nNot in RAM\n     ↓\nGet it from disk/swap\n     ↓\nPut it into RAM\n     ↓\nContinue execution\n\nBecause disk is slower than RAM, lots of swapping can make the machine very slow.\n\n6. Check Swap on Linux\nRun: free -h\n\nYou may see:\n\n               total   used   free\nMem:            16Gi    10Gi   1Gi\nSwap:            4Gi     0Gi   4Gi\n\nHere:\n\nRAM  = 16 GB\nSwap = 4 GB\n\n\nYou can also run:\nswapon --show \nThis shows configured swap areas.\n\n\n7. Important interview question\n\"If my server has 16 GB RAM and 4 GB swap, does it have 20 GB RAM?\"\nNo.\n\nIt has:\nPhysical RAM = 16 GB\nSwap         = 4 GB\n\nSwap is disk-backed storage, not physical RAM.\nThink:\n\n16 GB actual desk\n+\n4 GB cupboard\n\n≠\n\n20 GB desk\n\n\n8. Now the important production scenario\nImagine your Django application is running on an EC2 server.\n\nEC2\n┌──────────────────────┐\n│ RAM = 8 GB           │\n│                      │\n│ Django               │\n│ Nginx                │\n│ Celery               │\n│ Other processes      │\n└──────────────────────┘\n\n\nSuddenly Django starts consuming a lot of memory.\nLinux may first try to manage memory pressure using reclaimable memory, including filesystem cache, and potentially swap if configured and appropriate.\nBut suppose memory pressure becomes extreme.\nEventually:\n\nRAM\n████████████████\nVERY HIGH PRESSURE\n\nAnd Linux cannot satisfy an allocation.\n\nNow we reach our next concept:\n\n🚨 OOM Killer\nOOM = Out Of Memory\n\n\nThe OOM killer is Linux's mechanism for terminating processes when the system cannot satisfy memory demands.\n\nConceptually:\n\n\nApplication needs memory\n        ↓\nLinux tries to reclaim memory\n        ↓\nSwap may be used if available/configured\n        ↓\nStill cannot satisfy memory allocation\n        ↓\nOOM situation\n        ↓\nLinux may kill a process\n\nThe goal is basically to recover enough memory so the system can continue operating.\n\n\n🧠 Don't memorize the details yet\n\nFor today, make sure these are crystal clear:\n\nPage Cache\n\nSpare RAM can be used to cache file data to make future disk reads faster.\n\nSwap\n\nDisk space used to hold some memory pages when RAM is under pressure.\n\nOOM\n\nThe system cannot satisfy memory allocation demands, so Linux may kill a process to recover memory.\n\nAnd the overall picture:\n\n\n                RAM\n                 │\n        ┌────────┴────────┐\n        │                 │\n   Application       Page Cache\n        │\n        ↓\n   Memory pressure\n        │\n        ↓\n      Swap\n        │\n        ↓\n   Still insufficient?\n        │\n        ↓\n    OOM Killer\n\n\nNext step\n\nNow we can connect this directly to Docker:\n\nLinux memory\n     ↓\nDocker container\n     ↓\nContainer memory limit\n     ↓\ncgroups\n     ↓\nContainer gets OOMKilled\n\nThat's where these concepts become very practical for a backend/DevOps engineer.",
