      "created": 1790707747633,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "🎯 The simplest picture\nRemember this:",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "🎯 The simplest picture\nRemember this:",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "mpFeD7sFtFFE-3SGrKirr",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 577.4387146898734,
      "y": 27542.887612313432,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5Q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1612658562,
      "version": 12,
      "versionNonce": 1754870594,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790707788130,
      "created": 1790707761983,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "             PROCESS A\n          \"My address 100\"\n                 │\n                 ↓\n          Virtual Memory\n                 │\n                 ↓\n          Physical RAM\n          Address 2500\n\n\n             PROCESS B\n          \"My address 100\"\n                 │\n                 ↓\n          Virtual Memory\n                 │\n                 ↓\n          Physical RAM\n          Address 7000\n\n\nBoth processes can happily use address 100.\nThey don't conflict because 100 is a virtual address, not a physical RAM\naddress.\n\nOne sentence to remember:\nVirtual memory is like giving every process its own numbering system,\nwhile the OS maintains the mapping from that numbering system to the\nreal RAM.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "             PROCESS A\n          \"My address 100\"\n                 │\n                 ↓\n          Virtual Memory\n                 │\n                 ↓\n          Physical RAM\n          Address 2500\n\n\n             PROCESS B\n          \"My address 100\"\n                 │\n                 ↓\n          Virtual Memory\n                 │\n                 ↓\n          Physical RAM\n          Address 7000\n\n\nBoth processes can happily use address 100.\nThey don't conflict because 100 is a virtual address, not a physical RAM address.\n\nOne sentence to remember:\nVirtual memory is like giving every process its own numbering system, while the OS maintains the mapping from that numbering system to the real RAM.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "-z3rUO6fgdKAOdfbAqf0B",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 530.4387146898734,
      "y": 28345.012612313432,
      "width": 319,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
