      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Part 3 — Docker Memory Limits",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Part 3 — Docker Memory Limits",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "tFzNpgC94RHVlfacRPTMH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 567.6887146898734,
      "y": 28386.020424813432,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 6175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5S",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1170144350,
      "version": 184,
      "versionNonce": 1186059678,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790709149395,
      "created": 1790708666323,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This is where our Linux concepts start becoming directly useful for\nDocker and Kubernetes.\nFirst, let's connect what we already know\n\nLinux\n │\n ├── CPU\n ├── RAM\n ├── Processes\n └── Virtual Memory\n\nNow we put Docker into this picture.\n\n\n1. What is a Docker container really?\n    A container is basically a process (or group of processes) running\non the host Linux kernel, with isolation around things like processes,\nfilesystem, networking, and resources.\n\nYour EC2 Server\n─────────────────────────────\nLinux\n│\n├── Nginx process\n│\n├── PostgreSQL process\n│\n├── Docker\n│   │\n│   ├── Django container\n│   │     └── Django process\n│   │\n│   └── Celery container\n│         └── Celery process\n│\n└── Other processes\n\nThe containers are using the host's CPU and RAM.\nThere isn't a separate physical RAM stick inside each container.\n\n\n2. Here's the problem\nSuppose your EC2 server has: RAM = 8 GB\n\nYou start a Django container.\nNormally, without a memory limit, the container can potentially consume\na large amount of the host's available memory.\nImagine Django has a memory leak:\n\nDjango\n   ↓\n1 GB\n   ↓\n2 GB\n   ↓\n4 GB\n   ↓\n6 GB\n   ↓\n7 GB\n\nNow other processes are in trouble:\n8 GB RAM\n████████████████████\n\nDjango       → 7 GB\nNginx        → 500 MB\nSystem       → 500 MB\n\nThe whole machine is under serious memory pressure.\n\n\n3. Docker can put a limit on the container\nWe can tell Docker:\n\"This container is allowed to use at most 2 GB of memory.\"\n\nConceptually:\nHost RAM = 8 GB\n\n┌──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Linux                        │\n│                              │\n│ Django Container             │\n│ ┌──────────────────────────┐ │\n│ │ Memory limit = 2 GB      │ │\n│ └──────────────────────────┘ │\n│                              │\n│ Other applications           │\n└──────────────────────────────┘\n\nNow Django can use:\n500 MB\n1 GB\n1.5 GB\n2 GB\n\nBut when it tries to go beyond the configured limit, Docker/Linux's\nresource-control mechanisms can prevent it from continuing to consume\nunlimited memory.\n\n4. Why does Docker need this?\nImagine you have three containers:\n\nContainer A → Django\nContainer B → Celery\nContainer C → Redis\n\nServer: 8 GB RAM\n\nYou might want something like:\nDjango  → 2 GB limit\nCelery  → 2 GB limit\nRedis   → 1 GB limit\n\nThis provides resource isolation.\nOne workload shouldn't be able to consume all memory without\nrestriction.\nThis is especially important on production servers.\n\n\n5. But who actually enforces the limit?\nThis is where the next important concept comes in:\n\ncgroups\nShort for:\nControl Groups\n\nYou don't need to understand the internals yet.\nThink of cgroups as a Linux feature that lets us say:\n\"These processes belong to this group.\"\n\"And this group has these resource limits.\"\n\nFor example:\nLinux\n│\n├── Normal processes\n│\n└── cgroup\n      │\n      ├── Django\n      ├── Celery\n      │\n      └── Memory limit = 2 GB\n\nDocker uses Linux mechanisms such as cgroups to enforce resource\nconstraints on containers.\n\nSo the chain is:\nDocker\n   ↓\nContainer\n   ↓\nLinux cgroups\n   ↓\nMemory limit\n   ↓\nKernel enforces it\n\n6. What happens if Django reaches the limit?\nContainer memory limit = 2 GB\n\nDjango reaches:1.9 GB\nThen requests more memory.\n\nDjango\n  ↓\nNeeds more memory\n  ↓\ncgroup limit\n  ↓\nCannot satisfy allocation\n  ↓\nOOM situation inside cgroup\n  ↓\nProcess may be killed\n\nIn Docker you may see something like: OOMKilled\n\nThis means the container/process was killed because of an out-of-memory\ncondition under its memory constraints.\n\n\n7. This connects everything we've learned\n\n\n                 Linux\n                   │\n          ┌────────┴────────┐\n          ↓                 ↓\n        CPU                RAM\n                            │\n                     Virtual Memory\n                            │\n                          Pages\n                            │\n                       Memory pressure\n                            │\n                   ┌────────┴────────┐\n                   ↓                 ↓\n              Page Cache           Swap\n                                     │\n                                     ↓\n                              Still insufficient\n                                     │\n                                     ↓\n                                  OOM\n\n\nNow add Docker:\n\nDocker\n  │\n  ↓\nContainer\n  │\n  ↓\ncgroups\n  │\n  ↓\nMemory limit\n  │\n  ↓\nOOMKilled\n\nAnd this is exactly why the Linux memory concepts we're learning now\nmatter when you later work with Docker/Kubernetes.\n\n🧠 One thing I want you to understand before we go further\nSuppose:\nEC2 RAM = 8 GB\n\nContainer memory limit = 2 GB\nDoes Docker create 2 GB of RAM for that container?\n\nNo.\nIt doesn't reserve a separate 2 GB RAM stick.\nThe container still uses the host's physical memory.\nThe limit is simply a restriction enforced by Linux:\n\n\"Processes belonging to this container cannot consume memory beyond the\nconfigured limit.\"\n\nNext: cgroups in more detail\nWe'll now go one level deeper and understand how Linux actually groups\nprocesses and enforces CPU/memory limits.\nOnce cgroups are clear, Kubernetes Requests vs Limits becomes much\neasier.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This is where our Linux concepts start becoming directly useful for Docker and Kubernetes.\nFirst, let's connect what we already know\n\nLinux\n │\n ├── CPU\n ├── RAM\n ├── Processes\n └── Virtual Memory\n\nNow we put Docker into this picture.\n\n\n1. What is a Docker container really?\n    A container is basically a process (or group of processes) running on the host Linux kernel, with isolation around things like processes, filesystem, networking, and resources.\n\nYour EC2 Server\n─────────────────────────────\nLinux\n│\n├── Nginx process\n│\n├── PostgreSQL process\n│\n├── Docker\n│   │\n│   ├── Django container\n│   │     └── Django process\n│   │\n│   └── Celery container\n│         └── Celery process\n│\n└── Other processes\n\nThe containers are using the host's CPU and RAM.\nThere isn't a separate physical RAM stick inside each container.\n\n\n2. Here's the problem\nSuppose your EC2 server has: RAM = 8 GB\n\nYou start a Django container.\nNormally, without a memory limit, the container can potentially consume a large amount of the host's available memory.\nImagine Django has a memory leak:\n\nDjango\n   ↓\n1 GB\n   ↓\n2 GB\n   ↓\n4 GB\n   ↓\n6 GB\n   ↓\n7 GB\n\nNow other processes are in trouble:\n8 GB RAM\n████████████████████\n\nDjango       → 7 GB\nNginx        → 500 MB\nSystem       → 500 MB\n\nThe whole machine is under serious memory pressure.\n\n\n3. Docker can put a limit on the container\nWe can tell Docker:\n\"This container is allowed to use at most 2 GB of memory.\"\n\nConceptually:\nHost RAM = 8 GB\n\n┌──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Linux                        │\n│                              │\n│ Django Container             │\n│ ┌──────────────────────────┐ │\n│ │ Memory limit = 2 GB      │ │\n│ └──────────────────────────┘ │\n│                              │\n│ Other applications           │\n└──────────────────────────────┘\n\nNow Django can use:\n500 MB\n1 GB\n1.5 GB\n2 GB\n\nBut when it tries to go beyond the configured limit, Docker/Linux's resource-control mechanisms can prevent it from continuing to consume unlimited memory.\n\n4. Why does Docker need this?\nImagine you have three containers:\n\nContainer A → Django\nContainer B → Celery\nContainer C → Redis\n\nServer: 8 GB RAM\n\nYou might want something like:\nDjango  → 2 GB limit\nCelery  → 2 GB limit\nRedis   → 1 GB limit\n\nThis provides resource isolation.\nOne workload shouldn't be able to consume all memory without restriction.\nThis is especially important on production servers.\n\n\n5. But who actually enforces the limit?\nThis is where the next important concept comes in:\n\ncgroups\nShort for:\nControl Groups\n\nYou don't need to understand the internals yet.\nThink of cgroups as a Linux feature that lets us say:\n\"These processes belong to this group.\"\n\"And this group has these resource limits.\"\n\nFor example:\nLinux\n│\n├── Normal processes\n│\n└── cgroup\n      │\n      ├── Django\n      ├── Celery\n      │\n      └── Memory limit = 2 GB\n\nDocker uses Linux mechanisms such as cgroups to enforce resource constraints on containers.\n\nSo the chain is:\nDocker\n   ↓\nContainer\n   ↓\nLinux cgroups\n   ↓\nMemory limit\n   ↓\nKernel enforces it\n\n6. What happens if Django reaches the limit?\nContainer memory limit = 2 GB\n\nDjango reaches:1.9 GB\nThen requests more memory.\n\nDjango\n  ↓\nNeeds more memory\n  ↓\ncgroup limit\n  ↓\nCannot satisfy allocation\n  ↓\nOOM situation inside cgroup\n  ↓\nProcess may be killed\n\nIn Docker you may see something like: OOMKilled\n\nThis means the container/process was killed because of an out-of-memory condition under its memory constraints.\n\n\n7. This connects everything we've learned\n\n\n                 Linux\n                   │\n          ┌────────┴────────┐\n          ↓                 ↓\n        CPU                RAM\n                            │\n                     Virtual Memory\n                            │\n                          Pages\n                            │\n                       Memory pressure\n                            │\n                   ┌────────┴────────┐\n                   ↓                 ↓\n              Page Cache           Swap\n                                     │\n                                     ↓\n                              Still insufficient\n                                     │\n                                     ↓\n                                  OOM\n\n\nNow add Docker:\n\nDocker\n  │\n  ↓\nContainer\n  │\n  ↓\ncgroups\n  │\n  ↓\nMemory limit\n  │\n  ↓\nOOMKilled\n\nAnd this is exactly why the Linux memory concepts we're learning now matter when you later work with Docker/Kubernetes.\n\n🧠 One thing I want you to understand before we go further\nSuppose:\nEC2 RAM = 8 GB\n\nContainer memory limit = 2 GB\nDoes Docker create 2 GB of RAM for that container?\n\nNo.\nIt doesn't reserve a separate 2 GB RAM stick.\nThe container still uses the host's physical memory.\nThe limit is simply a restriction enforced by Linux:\n\n\"Processes belonging to this container cannot consume memory beyond the configured limit.\"\n\nNext: cgroups in more detail\nWe'll now go one level deeper and understand how Linux actually groups processes and enforces CPU/memory limits.\nOnce cgroups are clear, Kubernetes Requests vs Limits becomes much easier.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
