      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Part 4: cgroups",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Part 4: cgroups",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "DQlO74CNVuaX4VKOkV89L",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 558.8996521898734,
      "y": 34659.57901856343,
      "width": 1048.07421875,
      "height": 6025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 889553257,
      "version": 310,
      "versionNonce": 639235913,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790779985854,
      "created": 1790779408483,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "First, forget the word cgroup for a minute.\nWe already know:\n\nLinux\n ├── Process A\n ├── Process B\n ├── Process C\n └── Process D\n\nSuppose we have: RAM = 8 GB\nAnd Process A is our Django application.\n\nWhat if Django suddenly starts consuming all 8 GB?\nDjango → 8 GB\nOther processes → 😵\n\nWe need a way to tell Linux:\n\"These processes are allowed to use only this much memory.\"\nThat's what cgroups (Control Groups) help us do.\n\n1. Think of cgroup as a box\nImagine putting processes into a box:\n\n┌──────────────────────────────┐\n│        Django Group          │\n│                              │\n│  Django process              │\n│  Celery process              │\n│                              │\n│  Memory limit = 2 GB         │\n└──────────────────────────────┘\n\nLinux now knows:\nThese processes belong to this group.\n\nAnd we can configure resource limits for that group.\n\nFor example:\n\nMemory → maximum 2 GB\nCPU    → limited amount\n\nSo:\n\nProcesses\n    ↓\ncgroup\n    ↓\nResource limits\n    ↓\nLinux kernel enforces them\n\n\n2. Why \"group\"?\n\nBecause sometimes we don't want to control only one process.\nImagine your application has:\n\nDjango\nCelery\nGunicorn workers\n\nYou can put the relevant processes into a group:\n              Application\n                  │\n             ┌────┴────┐\n             ↓         ↓\n          Django     Celery\n             │         │\n             └────┬────┘\n                  ↓\n               cgroup\n                  │\n          Memory limit = 2 GB\n\nNow the limit applies to the group.\n\n\n3. Docker uses cgroups\n\nThis is where it becomes important for you.\nSuppose you run a container with a memory limit.\nConceptually:\n\n\nDocker\n   ↓\nCreate container\n   ↓\nPut container processes into a cgroup\n   ↓\nSet memory limit\n   ↓\nLinux kernel enforces limit\n\nSo Docker isn't independently controlling your RAM.\nLinux does the actual resource enforcement.\nDocker tells Linux:\n\"Create this resource-controlled group for this container.\"\n\n\n\n4. Let's use a real example\nSuppose your EC2 machine has: RAM = 8 GB\nYou run: Django container\n        Memory limit = 2 GB\n\nThe conceptual structure becomes:\n\nEC2\n│\n└── Linux\n     │\n     ├── Other processes\n     │\n     └── cgroup\n          │\n          └── Django container\n               │\n               └── Memory limit = 2 GB\n\n\nDjango might consume:\n500 MB\n   ↓\n1 GB\n   ↓\n1.5 GB\n   ↓\n2 GB\n\nAt this point it has reached its configured limit.\n\nIf it needs additional memory and the kernel cannot satisfy that allocation within the cgroup's\nconstraints, an OOM condition can occur and a process may be killed.\n\n\n5. cgroups aren't only for memory\nThis is important.\ncgroups can control/account for multiple resources, including:\nMemory -> Maximum memory\nCPU -> How much CPU time a group can receive\nPIDs -> Maximum number of processes\n\nThere are other controllers too.\nSo think:\ncgroup = Linux resource-control mechanism for a group of processes.\n\n\n6. cgroups vs Virtual Memory\n\nVirtual memory\nAnswers:\n\"What memory addresses can this process see, and where do they map?\"\n\nProcess\n   ↓\nVirtual address\n   ↓\nPhysical memory\n\ncgroups\nAnswers:\n\"How much resource can this group of processes consume?\"\nProcesses\n   ↓\ncgroup\n   ↓\nMemory / CPU / PID limits\n\nSo they solve different problems.\n\n\n\n7. Put everything we've learned together\nNow our Day 4 picture is becoming much clearer:\n\n                 Linux\n                   │\n          ┌────────┴────────┐\n          ↓                 ↓\n        CPU                RAM\n                            │\n                    Virtual Memory\n                            │\n                         Pages\n                            │\n                    Memory pressure\n                            │\n                  ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n                  ↓                   ↓\n             Page Cache             Swap\n                                      │\n                                      ↓\n                                Still insufficient\n                                      │\n                                      ↓\n                                     OOM\n\n\nThen Docker adds:\nDocker\n   ↓\nContainer\n   ↓\ncgroup\n   ↓\nMemory limit\n   ↓\nOOMKilled\n\n\n\n🎯 The 3 things you should remember\nDon't memorize the implementation yet.\n\nVirtual memory\nGives each process its own protected memory address space.\n\nSwap\nUses disk to hold some memory pages when RAM is under pressure.\n\ncgroups\nAllows Linux to control resources used by a group of processes.\n\nAnd this is the key Docker connection:\nDocker uses Linux cgroups to enforce resource limits on containers.\n\n\n\nNext concept: Kubernetes Requests vs Limits\nNow that you understand cgroups, Kubernetes will make much more sense.\nWe'll see why Kubernetes has:\n\nrequests:\n    \"I need approximately this much\"\n\nlimits:\n    \"Don't let me go beyond this\"\n\nand how that eventually leads to: Pod → OOMKilled",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "First, forget the word cgroup for a minute.\nWe already know:\n\nLinux\n ├── Process A\n ├── Process B\n ├── Process C\n └── Process D\n\nSuppose we have: RAM = 8 GB\nAnd Process A is our Django application.\n\nWhat if Django suddenly starts consuming all 8 GB?\nDjango → 8 GB\nOther processes → 😵\n\nWe need a way to tell Linux:\n\"These processes are allowed to use only this much memory.\"\nThat's what cgroups (Control Groups) help us do.\n\n1. Think of cgroup as a box\nImagine putting processes into a box:\n\n┌──────────────────────────────┐\n│        Django Group          │\n│                              │\n│  Django process              │\n│  Celery process              │\n│                              │\n│  Memory limit = 2 GB         │\n└──────────────────────────────┘\n\nLinux now knows:\nThese processes belong to this group.\n\nAnd we can configure resource limits for that group.\n\nFor example:\n\nMemory → maximum 2 GB\nCPU    → limited amount\n\nSo:\n\nProcesses\n    ↓\ncgroup\n    ↓\nResource limits\n    ↓\nLinux kernel enforces them\n\n\n2. Why \"group\"?\n\nBecause sometimes we don't want to control only one process.\nImagine your application has:\n\nDjango\nCelery\nGunicorn workers\n\nYou can put the relevant processes into a group:\n              Application\n                  │\n             ┌────┴────┐\n             ↓         ↓\n          Django     Celery\n             │         │\n             └────┬────┘\n                  ↓\n               cgroup\n                  │\n          Memory limit = 2 GB\n\nNow the limit applies to the group.\n\n\n3. Docker uses cgroups\n\nThis is where it becomes important for you.\nSuppose you run a container with a memory limit.\nConceptually:\n\n\nDocker\n   ↓\nCreate container\n   ↓\nPut container processes into a cgroup\n   ↓\nSet memory limit\n   ↓\nLinux kernel enforces limit\n\nSo Docker isn't independently controlling your RAM.\nLinux does the actual resource enforcement.\nDocker tells Linux:\n\"Create this resource-controlled group for this container.\"\n\n\n\n4. Let's use a real example\nSuppose your EC2 machine has: RAM = 8 GB\nYou run: Django container\n        Memory limit = 2 GB\n\nThe conceptual structure becomes:\n\nEC2\n│\n└── Linux\n     │\n     ├── Other processes\n     │\n     └── cgroup\n          │\n          └── Django container\n               │\n               └── Memory limit = 2 GB\n\n\nDjango might consume:\n500 MB\n   ↓\n1 GB\n   ↓\n1.5 GB\n   ↓\n2 GB\n\nAt this point it has reached its configured limit.\n\nIf it needs additional memory and the kernel cannot satisfy that allocation within the cgroup's constraints, an OOM condition can occur and a process may be killed.\n\n\n5. cgroups aren't only for memory\nThis is important.\ncgroups can control/account for multiple resources, including:\nMemory -> Maximum memory\nCPU -> How much CPU time a group can receive\nPIDs -> Maximum number of processes\n\nThere are other controllers too.\nSo think:\ncgroup = Linux resource-control mechanism for a group of processes.\n\n\n6. cgroups vs Virtual Memory\n\nVirtual memory\nAnswers:\n\"What memory addresses can this process see, and where do they map?\"\n\nProcess\n   ↓\nVirtual address\n   ↓\nPhysical memory\n\ncgroups\nAnswers:\n\"How much resource can this group of processes consume?\"\nProcesses\n   ↓\ncgroup\n   ↓\nMemory / CPU / PID limits\n\nSo they solve different problems.\n\n\n\n7. Put everything we've learned together\nNow our Day 4 picture is becoming much clearer:\n\n                 Linux\n                   │\n          ┌────────┴────────┐\n          ↓                 ↓\n        CPU                RAM\n                            │\n                    Virtual Memory\n                            │\n                         Pages\n                            │\n                    Memory pressure\n                            │\n                  ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n                  ↓                   ↓\n             Page Cache             Swap\n                                      │\n                                      ↓\n                                Still insufficient\n                                      │\n                                      ↓\n                                     OOM\n\n\nThen Docker adds:\nDocker\n   ↓\nContainer\n   ↓\ncgroup\n   ↓\nMemory limit\n   ↓\nOOMKilled\n\n\n\n🎯 The 3 things you should remember\nDon't memorize the implementation yet.\n\nVirtual memory\nGives each process its own protected memory address space.\n\nSwap\nUses disk to hold some memory pages when RAM is under pressure.\n\ncgroups\nAllows Linux to control resources used by a group of processes.\n\nAnd this is the key Docker connection:\nDocker uses Linux cgroups to enforce resource limits on containers.\n\n\n\nNext concept: Kubernetes Requests vs Limits\nNow that you understand cgroups, Kubernetes will make much more sense.\nWe'll see why Kubernetes has:\n\nrequests:\n    \"I need approximately this much\"\n\nlimits:\n    \"Don't let me go beyond this\"\n\nand how that eventually leads to: Pod → OOMKilled",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "BFdrkTtf44HfyH1SpKzeR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 475.28246468987345,
      "y": 40767.96573731343,
      "width": 396,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5W",
