      "seed": 86417191,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 1383669769,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790780128927,
      "created": 1790780123465,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Part 5: Kubernetes Requests & Limits",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Part 5: Kubernetes Requests & Limits",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Ykex1f0Z1QcBexLBW8Jzi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 528.7963575121391,
      "y": 40810.01261231343,
      "width": 970.9587634277342,
      "height": 6987.679687499999,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5X",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 235547463,
      "version": 296,
      "versionNonce": 7295049,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790781379564,
      "created": 1790780137610,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The key idea is very simple:\n\nKubernetes needs to know how much CPU and memory a container needs and how much it is\nallowed to use.\n\nSuppose we have a Kubernetes cluster:\n\nCluster\n│\n├── Node 1 → 8 GB RAM\n├── Node 2 → 16 GB RAM\n└── Node 3 → 8 GB RAM\nAnd we want to run our Django application.\n\n\n1. The problem Kubernetes has\n\nSuppose you say:\n\"Run my Django container.\"\n\nKubernetes needs to decide:\nWhich node should I put it on?\n\nImagine:\nNode 1\n8 GB RAM\nCurrently using 7 GB\n\nNode 2\n16 GB RAM\nCurrently using 4 GB\n\nNode 3\n8 GB RAM\nCurrently using 2 GB\n\nKubernetes needs some information about your workload.\nThat's where requests come in.\n\n\n2. Memory Request\nSuppose you configure:\n\nYAML\nresources:\n  requests:\n    memory: \"512Mi\"\n\nYou're basically telling Kubernetes:\n\"Please give this container a node where at least 512 MiB of memory is available for\nscheduling purposes.\"\n\nThis doesn't mean the container immediately consumes 512 MiB.\nIt's primarily a scheduling requirement.\nThink:\n\nDjango container\n\nRequest:\n    512 MiB\n\nMeaning:\n    \"I need this much for Kubernetes\n     to consider placing me on a node.\"\n\n3. Memory Limit\nNow suppose you configure:\n\nresources:\n  limits:\n    memory: \"1Gi\"\n\nThis means:\n\"The container should not be allowed to use more than 1 GiB under this memory limit.\"\n\nSo we might have:\nDjango\n\nRequest = 512 MiB\nLimit   = 1 GiB\n\nThink of it like a hotel booking:\n\nRequest\n↓\n\"I need at least this much capacity.\"\n\nLimit\n↓\n\"Don't let me go beyond this amount.\"\n\n\n\n4. Why have both?\n\nBecause they solve different problems.\nRequest → Scheduling\nKubernetes uses the request when deciding:\n\"Which node can accommodate this Pod?\"\n\nLimit → Maximum allowed resource usage\nThe limit says:\n\"How much can this container consume?\"\n\nSo:\n                Kubernetes\n                    │\n          ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n          ↓                   ↓\n       Request              Limit\n          ↓                   ↓\n    Scheduling           Maximum usage\n\n5. Example\nSuppose:\n\nresources:\n  requests:\n    memory: \"512Mi\"\n  limits:\n    memory: \"1Gi\"\n\nYour Django application starts.\nIt might actually use: 200 MiB \nNo problem.\n\nThen: 400 MiB\nStill fine.\nThen:\n700 MiB\n\nStill below the limit.\nThen:\n950 MiB\n\nStill below the limit.\nBut eventually: > 1 GiB\n\nNow we have a memory-pressure situation involving the container's configured limit.\nA process may be killed, resulting in the container being reported as:\nOOMKilled\n\n6. This connects directly to Docker\nRemember yesterday?\nDocker:\n\nDocker\n   ↓\ncgroup\n   ↓\nMemory limit\n\nKubernetes eventually uses the same underlying Linux resource-control mechanisms through\nthe container runtime.\nSo the overall chain is:\n\nKubernetes\n     ↓\nPod\n     ↓\nContainer\n     ↓\nLinux cgroup\n     ↓\nMemory limit\n     ↓\nKernel enforcement\nThat's why learning Linux before Kubernetes is useful.\n\n7. One important distinction\nSuppose your node has: 8 GB RAM\n\nAnd you have:\nPod A\nrequest = 2 GB\nlimit   = 4 GB\n\nPod B\nrequest = 2 GB\nlimit   = 4 GB\n\nIt does not mean Kubernetes immediately reserves:\n4 GB + 4 GB = 8 GB\n\nThe requests are primarily what Kubernetes uses for scheduling.\nThe limits describe the maximum allowed resource usage.\nSo Kubernetes might schedule these based on their requests, while actual usage can be\nlower.\n\n\n8. CPU is slightly different\nresources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: \"500m\"\n  limits:\n    cpu: \"1\"\n\nHere:\n1000m = 1 CPU\n500m  = 0.5 CPU\n\nSo:\nrequest = 500m\nlimit   = 1 CPU\n\nmeans approximately:\n\"Schedule me assuming I need half a CPU, and don't allow me to use more than one CPU.\"\n\nCPU behavior differs from memory when a container reaches its limit.\nMemory\nExceeding the memory limit can lead to: OOMKilled\n\nCPU\nCPU is generally throttled rather than the process being killed simply because it hit\nits CPU limit.\nThis distinction is very important for interviews.\n\n9. The complete picture\nNow you should be able to see the whole journey we've taken:\n\nPHYSICAL MACHINE\n       │\n       ↓\n     Linux\n       │\n       ├── CPU\n       │\n       └── RAM\n             │\n             ↓\n       Virtual Memory\n             │\n             ↓\n           Pages\n             │\n       ┌─────┴─────┐\n       ↓           ↓\n  Page Cache      Swap\n                     │\n                     ↓\n                    OOM\n                     \n       ↓\n    Docker\n       ↓\n   Container\n       ↓\n    cgroups\n       ↓\n Resource limits\n       │\n       ↓\n Kubernetes\n       ↓\n   Requests\n   Limits\n       ↓\n   Pod/Container\n       ↓\n    OOMKilled\n\n\nA Pod contains one or more containers, and resource requests/limits are specified for\ncontainers.\n\nKubernetes Node\n│\n├── Pod 1\n│    └── Django container\n│         request: 512Mi\n│         limit:   1Gi\n│\n├── Pod 2\n│    └── Celery container\n│         request: 256Mi\n│         limit:   512Mi\n│\n└── Pod 3\n     └── Nginx container\n          request: 128Mi\n          limit:   256Mi",
      "fontSize": 19.893749999999997,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The key idea is very simple:\n\nKubernetes needs to know how much CPU and memory a container needs and how much it is allowed to use.\n\nSuppose we have a Kubernetes cluster:\n\nCluster\n│\n├── Node 1 → 8 GB RAM\n├── Node 2 → 16 GB RAM\n└── Node 3 → 8 GB RAM\nAnd we want to run our Django application.\n\n\n1. The problem Kubernetes has\n\nSuppose you say:\n\"Run my Django container.\"\n\nKubernetes needs to decide:\nWhich node should I put it on?\n\nImagine:\nNode 1\n8 GB RAM\nCurrently using 7 GB\n\nNode 2\n16 GB RAM\nCurrently using 4 GB\n\nNode 3\n8 GB RAM\nCurrently using 2 GB\n\nKubernetes needs some information about your workload.\nThat's where requests come in.\n\n\n2. Memory Request\nSuppose you configure:\n\nYAML\nresources:\n  requests:\n    memory: \"512Mi\"\n\nYou're basically telling Kubernetes:\n\"Please give this container a node where at least 512 MiB of memory is available for scheduling purposes.\"\n\nThis doesn't mean the container immediately consumes 512 MiB.\nIt's primarily a scheduling requirement.\nThink:\n\nDjango container\n\nRequest:\n    512 MiB\n\nMeaning:\n    \"I need this much for Kubernetes\n     to consider placing me on a node.\"\n\n3. Memory Limit\nNow suppose you configure:\n\nresources:\n  limits:\n    memory: \"1Gi\"\n\nThis means:\n\"The container should not be allowed to use more than 1 GiB under this memory limit.\"\n\nSo we might have:\nDjango\n\nRequest = 512 MiB\nLimit   = 1 GiB\n\nThink of it like a hotel booking:\n\nRequest\n↓\n\"I need at least this much capacity.\"\n\nLimit\n↓\n\"Don't let me go beyond this amount.\"\n\n\n\n4. Why have both?\n\nBecause they solve different problems.\nRequest → Scheduling\nKubernetes uses the request when deciding:\n\"Which node can accommodate this Pod?\"\n\nLimit → Maximum allowed resource usage\nThe limit says:\n\"How much can this container consume?\"\n\nSo:\n                Kubernetes\n                    │\n          ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n          ↓                   ↓\n       Request              Limit\n          ↓                   ↓\n    Scheduling           Maximum usage\n\n5. Example\nSuppose:\n\nresources:\n  requests:\n    memory: \"512Mi\"\n  limits:\n    memory: \"1Gi\"\n\nYour Django application starts.\nIt might actually use: 200 MiB \nNo problem.\n\nThen: 400 MiB\nStill fine.\nThen:\n700 MiB\n\nStill below the limit.\nThen:\n950 MiB\n\nStill below the limit.\nBut eventually: > 1 GiB\n\nNow we have a memory-pressure situation involving the container's configured limit.\nA process may be killed, resulting in the container being reported as:\nOOMKilled\n\n6. This connects directly to Docker\nRemember yesterday?\nDocker:\n\nDocker\n   ↓\ncgroup\n   ↓\nMemory limit\n\nKubernetes eventually uses the same underlying Linux resource-control mechanisms through the container runtime.\nSo the overall chain is:\n\nKubernetes\n     ↓\nPod\n     ↓\nContainer\n     ↓\nLinux cgroup\n     ↓\nMemory limit\n     ↓\nKernel enforcement\nThat's why learning Linux before Kubernetes is useful.\n\n7. One important distinction\nSuppose your node has: 8 GB RAM\n\nAnd you have:\nPod A\nrequest = 2 GB\nlimit   = 4 GB\n\nPod B\nrequest = 2 GB\nlimit   = 4 GB\n\nIt does not mean Kubernetes immediately reserves:\n4 GB + 4 GB = 8 GB\n\nThe requests are primarily what Kubernetes uses for scheduling.\nThe limits describe the maximum allowed resource usage.\nSo Kubernetes might schedule these based on their requests, while actual usage can be lower.\n\n\n8. CPU is slightly different\nresources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: \"500m\"\n  limits:\n    cpu: \"1\"\n\nHere:\n1000m = 1 CPU\n500m  = 0.5 CPU\n\nSo:\nrequest = 500m\nlimit   = 1 CPU\n\nmeans approximately:\n\"Schedule me assuming I need half a CPU, and don't allow me to use more than one CPU.\"\n\nCPU behavior differs from memory when a container reaches its limit.\nMemory\nExceeding the memory limit can lead to: OOMKilled\n\nCPU\nCPU is generally throttled rather than the process being killed simply because it hit its CPU limit.\nThis distinction is very important for interviews.\n\n9. The complete picture\nNow you should be able to see the whole journey we've taken:\n\nPHYSICAL MACHINE\n       │\n       ↓\n     Linux\n       │\n       ├── CPU\n       │\n       └── RAM\n             │\n             ↓\n       Virtual Memory\n             │\n             ↓\n           Pages\n             │\n       ┌─────┴─────┐\n       ↓           ↓\n  Page Cache      Swap\n                     │\n                     ↓\n                    OOM\n                     \n       ↓\n    Docker\n       ↓\n   Container\n       ↓\n    cgroups\n       ↓\n Resource limits\n       │\n       ↓\n Kubernetes\n       ↓\n   Requests\n   Limits\n       ↓\n   Pod/Container\n       ↓\n    OOMKilled\n\n\nA Pod contains one or more containers, and resource requests/limits are specified for containers.\n\nKubernetes Node\n│\n├── Pod 1\n│    └── Django container\n│         request: 512Mi\n│         limit:   1Gi\n│\n├── Pod 2\n│    └── Celery container\n│         request: 256Mi\n│         limit:   512Mi\n│\n└── Pod 3\n     └── Nginx container\n          request: 128Mi\n          limit:   256Mi",
